# Summary
Introduced: the commit compacts control-plane history into sparse reverse deltas but does not update LocalClusterMap's historical route reconstruction semantics to match. The producer and ClusterRuntimeMapSnapshot reader now use reverse-delta semantics, while the local runtime map consumer still assumes dense historical snapshots.
The new control-plane runtime map representation no longer emits a PG route for every retained historical epoch. It emits only changed historical route records plus a baseline, and ClusterRuntimeMapSnapshot::reconstructed_pg_route_at_epoch was updated to interpret that sparse format by selecting the first historical route at or after the requested epoch, falling back to the current route. However, when a runtime map is converted into LocalClusterMap, only historical_pg_routes are copied, and LocalClusterMap::reconstructed_pg_route_at_epoch still uses the old dense-history algorithm: it selects the latest historical route at or before the requested epoch and only uses the current route when the requested epoch equals the current epoch. For example, if PG 30 moves from node 1 to node 2 at epoch 3 and the cluster later advances to epoch 10 without another PG 30 change, the sparse runtime map may contain the old node-1 route at epoch 2 and the current node-2 route at epoch 10. A local lookup for placement epoch 5 will incorrectly choose the epoch-2/node-1 route instead of the current node-2 route. Object payload metadata stores placement_cluster_epoch, so reads, cleanup, repair, and backfill of objects written during such intervening epochs can be directed to the wrong shard locations, causing NotFound/unavailability and potentially stale cleanup/backfill behavior.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [1b90025565eef8cb83eb8f7a85f8459c616dae34](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/1b90025565eef8cb83eb8f7a85f8459c616dae34) (`Align local sparse route reconstruction`).

`LocalClusterMap` now preserves the runtime map's canonical retained-epoch set instead of copying only its sparse route records. Both `ClusterRuntimeMapSnapshot` and `LocalClusterMap` call the same reverse-delta reconstruction helper: a retained historical lookup selects the first route delta at or after the requested epoch, falls back to the current route after the final delta, and rejects epochs before the PG introduction baseline. Local history is indexed by `(PG, epoch)` so this shared contract does not require expanding sparse history into dense snapshots.

The regression moves PG 30, advances the cluster through unrelated PG 31 changes, and compares runtime-map and local-map reconstruction for both PGs at every retained epoch. It specifically proves the formerly stale intervening epoch resolves to PG 30's new acting set and that PG introduction boundaries remain fail-closed. An existing historical Unix-shard fixture was also corrected so its historical route is older than its current map rather than relying on the old lookup accepting an impossible future-history state.

