## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the runtime-map producer/representation is sparse rather than dense for unchanged PGs across retained epochs.
- [x] Confirm `ClusterRuntimeMapSnapshot::reconstructed_pg_route_at_epoch` returns the correct current/future route for an intervening epoch after a PG move.
- [x] Confirm conversion into `LocalClusterMap` copies sparse `historical_pg_routes` without converting them to dense semantics or retaining `historical_cluster_epochs`.
- [x] Confirm `LocalClusterMap::reconstructed_pg_route_at_epoch` uses the old latest-route-at-or-before lookup and returns a stale pre-move PG route.
- [x] Confirm this affects normal local/runtime consumers via the `LocalClusterMap` reconstruction path; LLDB backtrace shows the stale route selected inside that method, and `StorageCluster::reconstructed_pg_route_at_epoch` delegates to this local map path.
## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC and an LLDB trace. The relevant code paths match the suspected mismatch: `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:1645-1665` emits sparse historical runtime-map PG routes; `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:3794-3834` reconstructs runtime-map history using the new reverse-delta rule, selecting the first historical route at or after the requested epoch and falling back to the current route; `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:1639-1643` copies only `runtime_map.historical_pg_routes()` into `LocalClusterMap`; and `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:2742-2765` still uses the old dense-history rule by searching for the latest historical route at or before the requested epoch. I added a PoC test that creates PG 30 on node 1, moves it to node 2, then advances the cluster with unrelated PG changes. The runtime map output was: `epochs: source=8 moved=9 intervening=10 current=11`, `historical pg30 routes: [(8, [NodeId(1)])]`, and `runtime pg30@10 -> [NodeId(2)]; local pg30@10 -> [NodeId(1)]`. This demonstrates that the runtime map reconstructs the intervening epoch correctly as node 2, while the converted local map reconstructs it incorrectly as stale node 1. I also ran LLDB non-interactively with a breakpoint in `LocalClusterMap::reconstructed_pg_route_at_epoch` at `cluster/local.rs:2764`; at the breakpoint, `pg_id = 30`, requested `cluster_epoch = 10`, but the selected `route` had `cluster_epoch = 8` and `primary_node_id = 1`, proving the stale <= historical lookup is the source of divergence. Valgrind was attempted but is not installed in the container, and this is a deterministic logic/correctness bug rather than a memory-safety crash.

