# Attack-path analysis
Final: ignore | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
The bug is validated and should be fixed as a storage correctness/availability regression, but it should not retain medium security criticality. Evidence shows the vulnerable code path is internal runtime-map conversion and refresh (crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:4808-4819, 5012-5037; crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:4360-4388). The stale route mismatch is real (crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:3794-3835 versus crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:2742-2765), and the PoC demonstrated runtime pg30@10 -> node2 while local pg30@10 -> node1. However, the attack preconditions require a control-plane-managed deployment, normal/admin PG placement changes, and historical object epochs; no repository evidence shows a remote attacker can force those changes, forge runtime maps, bypass SigV4 authentication, or access data across authorization boundaries. Under the project threat model focused on S3 HTTP authentication/authorization and data confidentiality/integrity, this is an operational availability/correctness issue rather than an accepted security vulnerability.
## Likelihood
low - Operational occurrence is plausible in control-plane-managed multihost deployments after PG moves, but attacker exploitation is unlikely because external clients do not control PG placement changes or runtime-map contents. The default documented mode is legacy-local with localhost binding.
## Impact
low - The proven effect is availability/correctness: objects written in affected historical epochs can be looked up through stale shard routes, causing NotFound/unavailability and potentially stale internal cleanup/backfill behavior. There is no demonstrated unauthorized data access, auth bypass, identity compromise, RCE, or secret disclosure.
## Assumptions
- Static review only; no cloud APIs or live deployment were queried.
- No Kubernetes, Terraform, Docker, or other deployment manifests were found in the checkout, so exposure is inferred from code and README defaults.
- The affected path requires a control-plane-managed frontend/storage deployment using runtime maps, not the default legacy-local single-process mode.
- The validation PoC is treated as credible evidence of the route reconstruction bug, but it demonstrates a deterministic storage correctness/availability failure rather than an attacker-driven security boundary bypass.
- argmin-s3 deployed with control-plane runtime-map refresh via ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_SOCKET_PATH
- a PG placement change followed by later epoch advancement without another change for that PG
- sparse historical runtime-map records retained for the relevant epochs
- read, cleanup, repair, backfill, or metadata operation referencing an object placement_cluster_epoch in the intervening epoch range
